{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Six hundred thousand footmen of the people that I am in its midst.</b> Besides the little ones, and You said, 'I will give them meat,' etc. 'If the flocks and herds be slaughtered for them and suffice for them' — far be it that Moses should doubt the power of the Creator to supply their needs; rather, he said, 'six hundred thousand footmen of the people that I am in its midst,' that is to say, they are lacking in faith and unworthy of a miracle, and by way of nature there is nothing that would suffice them: the flocks and herds that they have, which they brought out of Egypt, or any fish of the sea that they might catch on that side of the wilderness where they are — if these were slaughtered and gathered for them and sufficed them, would it suffice them? in wonderment." "<b>And behold,</b> the intent of Moses, peace be upon him, was that their request was not worthy of being fulfilled, neither by way of miracle nor by way of nature, for they were not worthy of it, since they were lacking in faith and did nothing but to test, as it is written (Psalms 78:18): ״וינסו אל בלבבם לשאול אכל לנפשם״; and not by way of nature, for by way of nature it would not suffice them. And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: 'Is HaShem's hand shortened to grant them their request and to supply them meat' — even by way of nature and circumstance, and not by way of miracle at all — 'now you shall see whether My word shall come to pass for you.' 'Gather Me seventy men of the elders of Israel,' for the spirit that is upon you I will apportion upon the seventy, and it will suffice them. This is what it says immediately: ״ויצא משה וידבר אל העם את דברי ה׳ ויאסוף שבעים איש״, etc., and ״וירד ה׳ בענן וידבר אליו ויאצל מן הרוח״; and it proceeds at length concerning the matter of Eldad and Medad. And just as your spirit is one and suffices for seventy men, the elders, so can I supply meat for that multitude of the people that you mentioned — six hundred thousand footmen — not by way of miracle at all but by way of nature and circumstance. This is what is written afterward: ״ורוח נסע מאת ה׳ ויגז שלוים מן הים״; and this was by way of nature, for He brought them out from the sea where they already were, and they were not a new creation. And this is the sense of ויגז שלוים, for they were gathered at the edge of the sea and He wafted them there, from the root of (Psalms 90:10): ״כי גז חיש ונעופה״." "<b>And behold,</b> He informed them in this passage that He, exalted be He, His hand is not shortened to provide them and supply them meat even by way of nature, besides flocks and herds and fish of the sea — thus did the Ramban, of blessed memory, explain. And what it says, ״היד ה׳ תקצר״, it should have been fitting to say ״הידי תקצר״, but it is like (Exodus 24:1): ״ואל משה אמר עלה אל ה׳״." "<b>And Rashi,</b> of blessed memory, explained: 'The flocks and herds be slaughtered for them' — in order that they die; this is what it said above, 'until it come out of your nostrils.' 'And suffice for them' — and let this eating supply them forever. 'And is this your praise?' — a parable of a horse to whom they say, 'Take a kor of barley and we will cut off your head.' The Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: 'Is HaShem's hand shortened?' If I do not give them meat, they will say that My hand is shortened. If it seems good to you that HaShem's hand appear shortened in their eyes, better that they perish, and a hundred like them, than that HaShem's hand be shortened before them even for one hour. 'Now you shall see whether My word shall come to pass for you.' Rabban Gamliel, son of Rabbi Yehudah the Nasi, says: it is impossible to grasp what they seek, since they are nothing but seekers of pretext — nothing will suffice them; in the end they will murmur against you. If you give them the meat of large cattle, they will say, 'We asked for small.' If small, they will say, 'We asked for large.' 'Wild beasts and fowl we asked for; fish and locusts.' Thus they will say that My hand is shortened." "<b>And the sage</b> Rabbi David Kimhi explained in the name of his father, of blessed memory: 'The flocks and herds be slaughtered for them' — for when they craved a craving, they had many flocks and herds, but they could not eat of them until they brought them to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, for the sake of the throwing of the blood and the burning of the fats and limbs, as it is written (Leviticus 17:3): ״איש איש מבית ישראל״, etc.; and furthermore, if the members of their household were not pure, they would not eat of them, as it is written (Leviticus 17:7 — but he cites it from the verse on slaughter in that chapter): ״אשר יאכל מזבח השלמים וטמאתו עליו ונכרתה״ (Leviticus 7:20). And therefore it says, 'the flocks and herds be slaughtered for them,' that is, that they would be able to slaughter in any place, and 'suffice for them' — truly it would suffice them; but if this were not so, how could they eat meat that would suffice them? The Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: 'Is HaShem's hand shortened? I will give them meat that will suffice, besides flocks and herds and fish.' Thus is written in the root מצא in Sefer Mikhlol, and it is a correct interpretation, except that the expression 'all the fish of the sea' does not sit well with the language according to the matter."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {4 "Rabbeinu Bahya's citation 'ובמקום אשר יאכל מזבח השלמים וטמאתו עליו ונכרתה' is labeled as Leviticus 17:7 in the Hebrew, but the verse 'אשר יאכל... ונכרתה' is actually Leviticus 7:20; rendered the citation as he wrote it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:45.522153944Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}